Diagnosis of the 2026-08-29 evening GATE_BLOCK cycling (20:47-21:03Z,
milestone .589). Root confirmed: exp6755 finished with
environment_grammar_targetable_rows=21 and exp6756's gate demanded >=24.
The gate failure was legitimate, but the value is frozen in a finished
artifact, so the 3 retries could never change the outcome. The
retirement then cascaded through exp6757..exp6760 at 3 GATE_BLOCK rows
per link: ~18 minutes of 2-minute cycling, 16 log rows, and 7 runnable
tasks delayed. The same shape recurred three times on 2026-08-28.

The competing GPU-contention theory was REFUTED by base rate: 3-4
wall-clock caps today versus 9 yesterday with no A/B running, and the
non-GPU exp6754 capped too. The GPU-1 A/B measurement is not implicated
and should not be paused. Wall-clock-cap retry accounting is
deliberately unchanged: removing it would let a task that caps 3x in a
row (observed 08-28) burn 80 minutes per extra retry.

The fix, three rules:
1. conductor_gates.evaluate_gates prefixes its failure summary with
   "gate-unsat(final): " when any FAILED gate read a FINISHED upstream
   artifact (_upstream_is_final: terminal status token, or terminal
   verdict prefix per the Verdict Terminal-Prefix Discipline). Missing,
   unreadable, running, blocked, and failed upstream artifacts stay
   transient because the upstream may re-run and rewrite them. The
   summary flows unchanged through the existing log_step call site.
2. pick_next_task retires a task on the FIRST GATE_BLOCK row whose
   details start with a terminal marker ("gate-unsat(final):" or
   "Pre-emptive skip: upstream retired"). Unmarked rows keep the
   3-strike budget (REQ-INFRA-085 semantics preserved).
3. pick_next_task closes the retired-task set transitively over
   gated_on edges, so a dead chain settles in one iteration instead of
   3 iterations per link.

Why not an activation-time lint: exclusion_manifest_lint's
REQUIRES_RETIRED_EXP checks the manifest of PRIOR-milestone
retirements; this chain died at runtime when exp6755 measured 21, which
no activation-time check can know. A chain-depth lint was considered
and rejected: it would fire on nearly every legitimate roadmap.

Proofs: 16 tests at the real entry points (evaluate_gates,
pick_next_task) using the incident's exact values; a read-only run over
the LIVE .589 roadmap shows the marker fires on exp6756 only; 11
mutations (each rule deleted or inverted) all RED, restores
byte-identical by cmp, final suite GREEN, zero decorative rules.
Adversarial sub-agent review applied: added lowercase-prefix and
80-char-truncation guards; its one RED finding was a false positive
(the needle matches the pre-existing status tuple).

Found at baseline and fixed in passing:
test_pick_next_task_source_includes_gate_block_in_failure_set had been
silently RED since the DOOMED_RERUN_BLOCK tuple extension (April) —
its source needle matched the pre-extension tuple. Needle updated.

Takes effect at the conductor's next process restart after merge; the
conductor is a long-lived --loop process.

